The problem describes a champagne pyramid where champagne is poured into the top glass. When a glass is full, overflow is split evenly between the two glasses beneath it.  The task is to determine how many glasses are full after t seconds.

The pyramid's structure can be represented by a 2D array. Each element [i][j] represents the amount of champagne in the glass at row i, column j (both 0-indexed). The size of the pyramid is determined by n, the number of rows.

The simulation can be performed iteratively, second by second up to t seconds. During each second, one unit of champagne is poured into the top glass. The overflow process needs to be carefully handled. When a glass overflows, half the overflow is distributed to each of the two glasses directly below. Since the pyramid levels start from 1 and go up to n, the indices in the code go form 0 to n-1.

Finally, the code counts the number of full glasses (i.e., glasses with champagne level >= 1.0).

