The code calculates the k-th lexicographical permutation of numbers from 1 to n and then counts how many numbers in the permutation are located at positions that are also lucky numbers (both the number and its index are lucky).

The solution cleverly handles large values of n by only generating the permutation for the last 15 numbers if n > 14. This is based on the observation that the first n-15 elements of the k-th permutation will be relatively close to their original index due to the limited effect of the lexicographical ordering given `k <= 10^9`.
The code consists of several functions:
*   `lucky(x)`: Checks if a number is lucky.
*   `Gen_lucky(n)`: Counts the number of lucky numbers less than or equal to n.
*   `Form(X, k)`: Generates the k-th permutation of the numbers in list X.

The code first precomputes factorials using an iterative method. Then, based on the value of n, it either calculates the entire permutation (if n <= 14) or only the last 15 elements.
If $n \le 14$: It directly forms the $k$-th permutation of the numbers from 1 to $n$ and counts the lucky positions.

If $n > 14$: It forms the $k$-th permutation of the numbers from $n-14$ to $n$ and calculates the quantity of lucky positions on the prefix $[1, n-15]$.
Specifically, it calculates how many lucky numbers are in the range [1, n-15]. The numbers in the generated permutation fall at indices $n-14, \ldots, n$. Thus, it needs to find the number of lucky numbers between 1 and $n-15$. This prefix part is counted separately by `Gen_lucky(ss)`. The number of lucky numbers on this range matches their index. Then it loops from $n-14$ to $n$ to count remaining lucky indices, but this time using positions from the newly formed permutation.
