The problem asks us to calculate the sum of "good" elements in a square matrix of size n x n, where n is odd. Good elements are defined as those on the main diagonal, the secondary diagonal, the middle row, and the middle column.  We iterate through each element of the matrix, check if it meets any of the "good" criteria, and add its value to a running sum. We need to be careful not to double-count elements that fall into multiple categories (e.g., the center element is on both diagonals, the middle row, and middle column).

The main diagonal consists of elements where the row index `i` equals the column index `j` (i.e., a[i][i]).
The secondary diagonal consists of elements where the sum of the row index `i` and the column index `j` equals `n - 1` (i.e., a[i][n-1-i]).
The middle row has index `n // 2`.
The middle column has index `n // 2`.

