### Reasoning

The problem asks us to compute the number of upward-pointing triangles after $n$ years, modulo $10^9 + 7$. The crucial observation is that the number of upward-pointing triangles $U(n)$ and downward-pointing triangles $D(n)$ can be expressed recursively. Specifically, each upward triangle at year $n$ turns into three upward triangles and one downward triangle at year $n+1$.  Each downward triangle at year $n$ also turns into three triangles of the same orientation(downward) and one triangle into the opposite orientation(upward). So, $U(n+1) = 3U(n) + D(n)$ and $D(n+1) = U(n) + 3D(n)$. Initially for $n=0$, we have $U(0) = 1$ and $D(0) = 0$.
Also note that since the total number of triangles at year $n$ is $4^n$, we can express $D(n) = 4^n -U(n)$. Hence, $U(n+1) = 3U(n) + 4^n-U(n) = 2U(n) + 4^n$.
We can represent the transitions with matrix multiplication letting
$
\begin{bmatrix}
U(n+1) \\ D(n+1)
\end{bmatrix} =
\begin{bmatrix}
3 & 1 \\ 1 & 3
\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}
U(n) \\ D(n)
\end{bmatrix}
$
Since $U(0)=1$ and $D(0) = 0$, we are looking for the top-left entry of the matrix
$
\begin{bmatrix}
3 & 1 \\ 1 & 3
\end{bmatrix}^n
\begin{bmatrix}
1 \\ 0
\end{bmatrix}
$
Thus we can compute
$
\begin{bmatrix}
3 & 1 \\ 1 & 3
\end{bmatrix}^n
$
using matrix exponentiation.  The final result will be the entry at (0,0) of the above matrix.

