The problem describes a scenario where a ninja needs to escape a canyon consisting of two walls. The ninja can move up, down, or jump to the other wall, which can be expressed as a graph search problem with constraints. Water levels are rising, so the ninja must also outpace them by moving efficiently. The goal is to check if there is a path to "escape," which in this case means surpassing the height of the canyon 'n.'

The key is to simulate the ninja's movements and keep track of visited states to avoid cycles and infinite loops. We can use Breadth-First Search (BFS) to explore possible moves. Each state in the BFS will include the current wall (left or right), the current height, and the current time (number of moves).

We mark areas flooded as unsafe based on the current time. A move is valid if the target area is not dangerous ("X" or flooded by the water level).

