The problem asks us to determine if there exist two camels, i and j, such that camel i spits at camel j and camel j spits at camel i. This occurs if camel i is at position x_i and spits a distance d_i, and camel j is at position x_j and spits a distance d_j, such that x_i + d_i = x_j and x_j + d_j = x_i. In other words, we need to check if there exist two lines in the input where x_i + d_i = x_j and x_j + d_j = x_i.

We can iterate through all pairs of camels (i, j) with i != j. For each pair, we check if the conditions `x[i] + d[i] == x[j]` and `x[j] + d[j] == x[i]` hold. If they do, we output "YES" and return. If we finish iterating through all pairs and haven't found such a pair, we output "NO".

