### Reasoning

The problem asks us to find the optimal order to pick up all the objects and bring them back to the handbag, minimizing the total travel time (squared Euclidean distance). The girl can carry at most two objects at a time. This can be modeled as a bitmask dynamic programming problem.

Let `dp[mask][last]` be the minimum time to pick up the objects represented by the `mask` (a bitmask where the i-th bit is 1 if object i has been picked up), ending at object `last` (or the handbag, which we represent as index 0).

The base case is `dp[0][0] = 0`, meaning no objects are picked up and we are at the handbag.

For transitions, we have two cases:

1.  The girl is carrying only one object (`last`). We can pick up another object `i` and go to `i`. The current mask `mask` does not contain the bit corresponding to object `i`, so `(mask & (1 << i)) == 0`.  Also the `i` should not be 0.
    `dp[mask | (1 << i)][i] = min(dp[mask | (1 << i)][i], dp[mask][last] + dist(last, i))`

2.  The girl is carrying two objects. We go back to the handbag (0). We iterate though all the already picked objects to be `last`. The cost will `dp[mask][last] + dist(last, 0)` after this operation, and we are back at the handbag location. In current case from handbag (0) picking up two objects `i` and `j`. The mask must not contains `i` and `j`'s bits. Then, we goes to `i`, `dp[mask | (1 << i) | (1 << j)][i] = min(dp[mask | (1 << i) | (1 << j)][i], dp[mask][0] + dist(0, i) + dist(i, j) )` and goes to `j` `dp[mask | (1 << i) | (1 << j)][j] = min(dp[mask | (1 << i) | (1 << j)][j], dp[mask][0] + dist(0, j) + dist(j, i))`

After calculating the `dp` table, the answer is the minimum of `dp[(1 << n) - 1][i] + dist(i, 0)` for all `i`. We also need to reconstruct the path. During DP we also keep a `pre[mask][last]` table, which stores the previous state where we came from.

