The problem asks us to find a way for Polycarp (student 1) to spread news to all other students, given each student *i* can send at most *a<sub>i</sub>* messages. Initially, only Polycarp knows the news. A student can only send a message if they know the news. We need to output the messages (sender, receiver) such that everyone eventually knows, or output -1 if impossible.

A simple breadth-first search (BFS) or depth-first search (DFS) approach should work. We can keep track of who knows the news. We start with Polycarp knowing. Then, we iterate through the students. If a student knows the news and has messages remaining to send, we can send them to any student who doesn't yet know the news.

If after iterating through all possible messages, some students still don't know, we output -1.

To optimize, we can keep track of which students have already received the news and only let eligible students send messages to those which haven't received the news.

